title:: Evaluating and Executing .scd Files (SC-IDE & CLI)
summary:: Guide for startup, evaluating, loading, and executing .scd files in SC-IDE and from the command line
categories:: Tutorials
related:: Classes/Document, Classes/Main#thisProcess, Classes/PathName, Classes/Platform, Classes/File, Classes/Interpreter

DESCRIPTION::
This guide summarizes methods for starting, evaluating, loading, and executing teletype::.SCD:: files within SC-IDE and from the command line.
It also covers techniques for modularizing code with relative paths and for automatic execution upon file opening or SuperCollider startup.

table::
## Method||Use Case||Returns||Notes
## startup.scd|| Global Boot||Depends on the file|| Runs every time SuperCollider starts
## code::/*RUN*/::
at the first line of an SCD||Auto-Run on Open||Depends on the file|| IDE specific:

runs code when file opens
## code::.load::|| Execute File|| Result of last line|| Standard way to run external code
## code::.loadRelative::|| Execute File|| Result of last line|| Best for portable projects
## Document.open|| Open Editor|| Document Object|| Opens file in IDE

(does not run code)
## sclang (CLI)|| Batch/Headless|| Exit Code|| Runs outside of SC-IDE

(terminal or prompt)
::

strong::Common Pitfalls::

list::
## strong::Document Path:::

code::resolveRelative:: and code::loadRelative:: will return code::nil:: or code::WARNING:: if the current document has not been saved, as it does not yet have a path on disk.

## strong::Infinite Loops:::

Be cautious when using code::.load:: in a way that the loaded file references itself, which can lead to infinite loops.
::


section:: Startup File (Global Auto-Run)

To make code execute automatically whenever SuperCollider launches (regardless of which file is opened), use the teletype::startup.scd:: file in code::Platform.userAppSupportDir::.
In SC-IDE, you can also open it directly from the menu bar by selecting teletype::File:: → teletype::Open startup file::.
If you are not using SC-IDE, follow these steps instead:

numberedlist::
## Find where your startup file is located:
code::
Platform.userAppSupportDir.openOS
::

## Open the startup file for editing (create the file if it doesn't exist):

code::
(
var path = Platform.userAppSupportDir +/+ "startup.scd";
if(File.exists(path).not) { File.use(path, "w") };
path.openDocument
)
::
::

Examples of useful startup code (inside teletype::startup.scd::):
list::
## To load other files:
code::
"~/Documents/SuperColliderProject1/main.scd".standardizePath.load;
"~/Documents/SuperColliderProject2/main.scd".standardizePath.load;
::
## To set in/out number of audio bus channels:
code::
s.options.numInputBusChannels = ~nI = 8;
s.options.numOutputBusChannels = ~nO = 8;
::
## To set memory size:
code::
s.options.memSize = 2.pow(21);
::
## To set audio device:
code::
s.options.device = "Fireface 800 (3F0)";
::
::


section:: Auto-Run on Document Open (SC-IDE)

In the SuperCollider IDE, you can make a specific file execute automatically when it is opened.

strong::How to use:::
Place the string code::/*RUN*/:: on the very first line of the document.

code::
/*RUN*/
"Hello, this SCD file runs automatocally when opened".postln;
s.boot;
::

note::
list::
## This feature is specific to the SuperCollider IDE editor.
It is useful for performance patches or tools that should start immediately when the file is opened.

## If this does not work, follow these steps to resolve the issue:
numberedlist::
## (Optional) Check the value of code::Document.autoRun:::
code::
Document.autoRun
::
The default value is teletype::true::, but it may have been changed to teletype::false:: by code evaluation.
## Set the value of code::Document.autoRun:: to teletype::true:::
code::
Document.autoRun_(true)
::
## (Optional) Verify the value of code::Document.autoRun:: again:
code::
Document.autoRun
::
::
::
::

section:: Loading Files (Evaluating entire code blocks in specific SCDs without opening them)

This is the most common way to execute code stored in other files from within a running program.
warning:: The file must be saved to disk for relative paths to work.::

list::
##Load using absolute path
code::
"/full/path/to/filename.scd".load;
::
##Load using relative paths (Recommended for projects)
code::
"filename.scd".loadRelative;            // Search in the same directory
"../lib/utils.scd".loadRelative;        // Search in parent/lib directory
::
##Alternative relative path construction (Manual)
code::
(
var path = thisProcess.nowExecutingPath.dirname;
(path ++ "modules/synthdefs.scd").load;
)
::
::

subsection:: Variable Scope in Loaded Files

When working across multiple teletype::SCD:: documents, it is helpful to properly use the following variable types according to the appropriate scopes:

list::
## strong::Environment variables:: (e.g., ~myVar) and strong::interpreter variables:: (e.g., a) persist and are shared between the main document and any loaded file.
## strong::Local variables:: (e.g., var myVar) are scoped only to the file being loaded and are not visible to the main document.
::

code::
// in main.scd
~globalSetting = 100;
"config.scd".loadRelative;
~loadedSetting.postln; // Works if defined in config.scd

// in config.scd
var localCalc = 10 * ~globalSetting; // uses ~globalSetting defined in main.scd for calculation
~loadedSetting = localCalc;          // exports result to environment
::

section:: Opening SCD Files in SC-IDE

Use these methods to open a file in the text editor without evaluating its code immediately.
list::
## Open using absolute path:
code::
Document.open("/full/path/to/filename.scd")
::
or
code::
"/full/path/to/filename.scd".openDocument
::

## Open using a relative path (the file must be saved first):
code::
// Opens a document located in the same folder as the file containing the evaluated code:
"filename.scd".resolveRelative.openDocument;

// Opens a document located in the parent folder of the file containing the evaluated code:
"../other.scd".resolveRelative.openDocument;
::
::

section:: Command Line Execution

SuperCollider (sclang) can be run from a terminal or command prompt.
This is useful for installation art, headless systems (e.g., Raspberry Pi), or batch processing.

list::
## Basic usage:
code::
sclang filename.scd        // on Linux and macOS
sclang.exe filename.scd    // on Windows
::
warning::
list::
## To run code::sclang:: or code::sclang.exe:: directly, the path to code::sclang:: must be included in the system PATH environment variable.
## To run a file by its filename only (without specifying the full path), the CLI command must be executed from the same folder as the file.
::
::
## With configuration file:
code::
sclang -l sclang_conf.yaml filename.scd     // on Linux and macOS
sclang.exe -l sclang_conf.yaml filename.scd // on Windows
::
warning::
The path to teletype::sclang_conf.yaml:: must be explicitly specified if the command is executed outside of code::Platform.userConfigDir::.
::
::

subsection::Setting up 'sclang' in PATH

list::
## strong::macOS:::

Add this to your shell config (~/.zshrc or ~/.bash_profile):
code::
export PATH="/Applications/SuperCollider.app/Contents/MacOS:$PATH"
::

## strong::Windows:::
Add the installation folder to your System PATH environment variable:
code::
C:\Program Files\SuperCollider-3.x.x\
::

## strong::Linux:::
Usually installed in code::/usr/bin/:: or code::/usr/local/bin/:: by default.
::

section:: Advanced: Compile String or File

For advanced use cases where you need to read code as data or compile it into a function without immediately running it.

code::
// Read file as a String
d = File.readAllString("data.txt".resolveRelative);

// Compile file into a Function (does not execute yet)
f = thisProcess.interpreter.compileFile("script.scd".resolveRelative);
f.value; // Execute now
::

section::Examples

subsection:: Example 1: Auto-running installation file

numberedlist::
## Save an teletype::SCD:: file with the following example code:
code::
/*RUN*/
s.waitForBoot({
    "Installation Started".postln;
    { SinOsc.ar(440, 0, 0.1) }.play;
});
::
## Open the file using SC-IDE.
## The entire code in the opened file is immediately evaluated upon opening.
::

subsection:: Example 2: Project Loader Script (main.scd)

code::
(
// Load modules relative to this file
"audio/synths.scd".loadRelative;
"music/patterns.scd".loadRelative;
"interface/gui.scd".loadRelative;
)
::

subsection:: Example 3: Headless execution (Shell Script)

warning::
The path to sclang must be registered in the operating system’s terminal/command‑line environment so that it is recognized as a valid command.
::

subsubsection:: Linux and macOS

If the filename is teletype::run.sh::, open a terminal in the same folder and type:

code::
./run.sh
::
Contents of teletype::run.sh:: should be:
code::
#!/bin/bash
cd "$(dirname "$0")"
sclang installation.scd
::

subsubsection:: Windows

If the filename is teletype::run.bat::, open a Command Prompt in the same folder and type:

code::
run.bat
::

Contents of teletype::run.bat:: should be:

code::
@echo off
cd /d %~dp0
sclang installation.scd
::
